## Product-owner decision and exact-head proof

As the feature owner, I am explicitly approving retirement of the extension tab-copilot, page-share, prompt box, context-menu/shortcut, and side-panel workflows in this PR. This is intentional product simplification, not an accidental bootstrap regression. The extension's contract is browser automation transport only. Agent prompts continue through normal OpenClaw channels; direct remote-Gateway pairing remains available under Advanced manual pairing. Existing valid relay pairings and stored access modes remain authoritative, retired copilot state is removed narrowly, and the changelog/docs communicate the upgrade.

The native-host boundary has now been independently inspected and exercised on the exact head:

- deterministic IDs are derived only from canonical OpenClaw-owned unpacked realpaths and verified against Chrome Secure Preferences;
- manifests contain exact `chrome-extension://<id>/` origins, never wildcards or a manifest key;
- the one-shot host validates its owned manifest/launcher, caller origin, strict object shape, fatal UTF-8, bounded native-endian framing, and local/browser-node topology before returning a pairing;
- direct remote keys are never returned by native bootstrap, and keys/pairing strings are absent from status, diagnostics, argv, and logs;
- Max-P1 Codex autoreview was clean across two complete chunks, TruffleHog was clean, and source-blind behavior validation plus macOS/Linux Chromium E2E passed.

Signed-in Chrome proof also completed: the stable pre-registered extension auto-paired without popup setup, Settings reported automatic bootstrap ready and All tabs, the development relay reached Connected, and mcporter with policy `require` reported relay success/HTTP 200, selected a known public page, wrote a transient marker, and cleared it without permitting legacy fallback.

